@flexiarg or3/consent-bounded-analysis
@title Consent-Bounded Analysis
@audience qualitative researchers, workshop facilitators, data stewards, ethics leads
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🔒/界 🧭/守]
@see or3/count-every-card-back, or3/openness-without-exposure

! conclusion: Consent-Bounded Analysis

  + context: You are deciding where the material people gave you is allowed to be processed.

  + IF:
    You hold contributions gathered from identifiable people — workshop cards, interview transcripts, photographs of participants — and you face an analysis task (theming, coding, summarising) that a third-party AI platform would do quickly.

  + HOWEVER:
    Pasting the material into a general-purpose platform is now the path of least resistance, and it is a disclosure. The consent you obtained covers your project, not an external service's processing or retention; participants who agreed to help you did not agree to that, and in most cases were never asked.

  + THEN:
    Decide where the material may be processed before you collect it, write that decision into the participant information, and hold to it — coding by hand or on institutionally governed infrastructure where the answer is no. Where you do want to use such tools, obtain consent for that specific use in those terms. Apply the same test to images and recordings: consent to be photographed at a workshop is not consent to publication or licensing.

  + BECAUSE:
    A decision made in advance is a research-design choice you can state and defend; the same decision made mid-analysis, under deadline, is a disclosure nobody authorised. Communities that discover their material was handled this way do not come back, and the cost lands on the next project as much as on this one.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Add a line to the data management plan naming which platforms are and are not permitted for this material.]
    next[Check that photo consent forms distinguish participation, publication, and open licensing.]
